oke is not counted in it","the design variable crush_stiffness is not used (the crush force comes from the rails' tubes)","rigid wall; the motion up to the standstill, the rebound is not followed","comparison on the demo design: A (this tool: two rails folding at 48.4 kN each, 96.7 kN, 15.3 g while crushing, crush 489 mm = 80 mm bumper + 409 mm folds, 81.1 ms to the stop, mean 14.0 g (speed / stop time), battery mount force 7.5 kN, occupant space left +256 mm of 745 mm, not bottomed: the usable length is 819 mm), the energy balance (crash.plastic_crush: the same 489 mm, 15.3 g and 81.1 ms) and B (the K2 crash_beam tool: beams with plastic hinges, axial force up to A fy, no local buckling of the tubes: 536 mm, peak 64.0 g, mean 11.9 g, 94.9 ms, battery mount force 15.4 kN, occupant_space_margin +194 mm). B's peak is about the rails' squash load A fy (531 mm^2 x 245 MPa = 130 kN per rail, 2.7 times the folding mean force), since without local buckling a tube does not fold; A's folding force is a mean over the folds and leaves out the first peak, so A's peak equals its mean. B crushes further at a lower mean deceleration, so after its peak its force is on average below A's folding force (how, in B's hinges, was not looked at). A stops the crush at 70 % of the crush zone's length (the folds' solid length) and then a stiff spring takes the rest (crush_bottomed); B has no such limit; neither bottoms out here. The occupant space margins compare: A's +256 mm and B's occupant_space_margin +194 mm (as K2 gave it; B crushes 47 mm further)."],"scene":[{"kind":"wall","label":"剛体の壁","position_mm":[515.0,0.0,1360.9125],"size_mm":[200.0,2550.0,2721.825]}],"wall_time_s":0.6203044999856502,"analysis_id":"ana-0001"}